@ 17.7.2.2 - West Germanic to Old Low Franconian
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Old Dutch”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Old_Dutch>; and Wikipedia contributors (2014), “Germanic umlaut”. Wikipedia, the Free Encyclopedia. <https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Germanic_umlaut> Final obstruents devoice Some vowel reduction seems to have occurred in unstressed syllables
	e:[+long] o:[+long] > ie uo
	ai au > e:[+long] o:[+long]
	h > ∅ / #_C
	jan > en / CC_#
	j > ∅ / CC_
	h > ∅ / V_V
	xs > s:[+long]
	a > ɒ? ( / _ɫ
	ɒ? ( > o) / _ɫ
	ai u > ei ʏ / _(C…){{ i, i:[+long] },j} (short only; in the case of:[ʏ] at least this was not yet phonemic)
	a > ɛ / _(C…){{ i, i:[+long] },j} (conjectured based on date from the “Germanic umlaut” article)
	u:[+long] > ʉw / _V (probably, in most areas)
	u:[+long] > ʊw / _V (probably, in areas that did not undergo the above change, such as Limburg)
	u:[+long] > ʉ:[+long] (probably, in areas with u:[+long] / _V)
	ʉ:[+long] (probably, in areas with u:[+long] > ʉw / _V)
	ei ou > e:[+long] o:[+long] (except in southeastern dialects; *ei as a result of the umlaut of *ai was not affected)